    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "EVIDENCE",
      "narration_text": "Here's the mechanism. Elastic scattering is just Rayleigh scattering — boring. But inelastic scattering is where the magic happens. In Stokes Raman scattering, the photon gives up energy to the molecule. It comes back lazier, with a longer wavelength. In Anti-Stokes, it steals energy and comes back hotter.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B02_PhotonCollision",
          "file": "scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 15,
      "actual_duration_s": 16.73,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim:B02_PhotonCollision",
        "at": "2026-09-04T12:31:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "EVIDENCE",
      "narration_text": "Because every chemical bond vibrates at a specific frequency, the exact energy shift of that photon gives you a perfect molecular fingerprint. It's non-destructive, water doesn't ruin the reading, and you can shoot a laser right through a plastic bottle to test what's inside.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
          "props": {
            "artifactTitle": "The Fingerprint",
            "artifactHeading": "Why we use it",
            "artifactLines": [
              "Measures specific vibrational modes.",
              "Non-destructive and non-contact.",
              "Water produces a very weak signal (unlike FTIR)."
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 15,
      "actual_duration_s": 15.45,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-09-04T12:31:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "PROBLEM",
      "narration_text": "But there's a catch. The signal is incredibly weak. Just one in ten million photons. And if your sample fluoresces under a visible laser, that glow will completely swamp your tiny Raman signal. You're left trying to hear a whisper at a rock concert.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
          "props": {
